,"self_ref":"#/texts/32","text":"M"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"+1 (","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":495.50002697333326,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":470.27994,"r":488.257546246208,"t":504.0599310799999},"charspan":[0,4],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/33","text":"+1 ("},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"N","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":495.9094916599999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":488.27994,"r":495.64133798,"t":503.76404053999994},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/34","text":"N"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"= 30).","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":495.50002697333326,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":499.19984,"r":524.1952201759765,"t":504.0599310799999},"charspan":[0,6],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/35","text":"= 30)."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Figure 3: The L 2 -errors versus N and M +1 of Example 1 using the spectral method.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":467.6725279999999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":99.47983999999997,"r":512.6617319632741,"t":480.33754549999986},"charspan":[0,83],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/36","text":"Figure 3: The L 2 -errors versus N and M +1 of Example 1 using the spectral method."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"From Figure 3(a), it is evident that the proposed method exhibits spectral accuracy concerning the polynomial degree N . As the degree N increases to 6, the L 2 -errors approach the machine epsilon, indicating the convergence of the numerical solution to the exact solution. Similarly, in Figure 3(b), it can be observed that when the angular discretization number M +1 is small (e.g., M = 1), the L 2 -errors also approach the machine epsilon. This occurs because the exact solution is a sixth-degree polynomial solely in the spatial variable x and is entirely independent of the angular variable µ . As a result, selecting N = 6 and M = 1 enables the L 2 -error to reach machine epsilon.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":345.272725,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.19961999999998,"r":532.8554347637,"t":450.981825},"charspan":[0,689],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/37","text":"From Figure 3(a), it is evident that the proposed method exhibits spectral accuracy concerning the polynomial degree N . As the degree N increases to 6, the L 2 -errors approach the machine epsilon, indicating the convergence of the numerical solution to the exact solution. Similarly, in Figure 3(b), it can be observed that when the angular discretization number M +1 is small (e.g., M = 1), the L 2 -errors also approach the machine epsilon. This occurs because the exact solution is a sixth-degree polynomial solely in the spatial variable x and is entirely independent of the angular variable µ . As a result, selecting N = 6 and M = 1 enables the L 2 -error to reach machine epsilon."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Furthermore, by comparing Figure 2(b) and Figure 3(a), it is evident that for problems with a higher regularity of the exact solution, the spectral method requires fewer degrees of freedom compared to the Hermite WENO fast sweeping method to achieve the same level of accuracy.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":304.592725,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.19999999999999,"r":532.7670870906339,"t":342.621825},"charspan":[0,277],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/38","text":"Furthermore, by comparing Figure 2(b) and Figure 3(a), it is evident that for problems with a higher regularity of the exact solution, the spectral method requires fewer degrees of freedom compared to the Hermite WENO fast sweeping method to achieve the same level of accuracy."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"5.2 Example 2","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":275.6369047999999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.19999999999999,"r":172.07920231731197,"t":286.40854},"charspan":[0,13],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/39","text":"5.2 Example 2"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In this test, we solve the absorbing-scattering transfer problem described by the equation (1.1) with","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":241.23292500000002,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.19999999999999,"r":532.8327308,"t":265.701825},"charspan":[0,101],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/40","text":"In this test, we solve the absorbing-scattering transfer problem described by the equation (1.1) with"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"Σ t = 22000 , Σ s = 1 ,","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":226.72739249999995,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":253.80000000000004,"r":358.1018907,"t":238.58182499999998},"charspan":[0,23],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/41","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"s ( x, µ ) = -4 πµ 3 cos 3 πx sin πx +Σ t ( µ 2 cos 4 πx + const ) -Σ s ( const + cos 4 πx 3 ) .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":193.8874639999999,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":118.44007000000005,"r":493.5818207,"t":221.6178625},"charspan":[0,96],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/42","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Here const = 10 -14 is a small positive constant which is used to ensure the source term to be nonnegative. The computational domain is D = (0 , 1). The boundary condition is given as follows","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":150.39258500000005,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.19989999999996,"r":532.9634930999221,"t":190.1776125},"charspan":[0,191],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/43","text":"Here const = 10 -14 is a small positive constant which is used to ensure the source term to be nonnegative. The computational domain is D = (0 , 1). The boundary condition is given as follows"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"{ ϕ (0 , µ ) = µ 2 + const, if µ > 0 , ϕ (1 , µ ) = µ 2 + const, if µ < 0 .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":119.51991309999994,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":224.63999999999993,"r":386.0618206999998,"t":152.37752250000005},"charspan":[0,75],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/44","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"For this problem, we have the exact solution given as [25]","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":101.07269499999995,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":79.19991999999974,"r":354.3818207,"t":111.98182499999996},"charspan":[0,58],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/45","text":"For this problem, we have the exact solution given as [25]"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"ϕ ( x, µ ) = µ 2 cos 4 πx + const.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":76.592625,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":237.48000000000002,"r":374.54151453637405,"t":89.73771250000004},"charspan":[0,34],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/46","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"M","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":500.19466029999995,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":420.71978999999993,"r":427.6597603799999,"t":505.7538097},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/47","text":"M"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"16","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":46.592625,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":300.59986000000004,"r":311.4544145,"t":57.501724999999965},"charspan":[0,2],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/48","text":"16"}],"version":"1.10.0"}